23	/**
24	 The {@link Safelist}-based HTML cleaner. Use to ensure that end-user provided HTML contains only the elements and attributes
25	 that you are expecting; no junk, and no cross-site scripting attacks!
26	 <p>
27	 The HTML cleaner parses the input as HTML and then runs it through a safelist, so the output HTML can only contain
28	 HTML that is allowed by the safelist.
29	 </p>
30	 <p>
31	 It is assumed that the input HTML is a body fragment; the clean methods only pull from the source's body, and the
32	 canned safelists only allow body-contained tags.
33	 </p>
34	 <p>
35	 Rather than interacting directly with a Cleaner object, generally see the {@code clean} methods in {@link org.jsoup.Jsoup}.
36	 </p>
37	 <p>
38	 A Cleaner may be reused across multiple documents and shared across concurrent threads once its {@link Safelist} has
39	 been configured. The cleaner uses the supplied safelist directly, so later safelist changes affect later cleaning
40	 calls. If you need a variant of an existing configuration, use {@link Safelist#Safelist(Safelist)} to make a copy.
41	 </p>
42	 */
43	public class Cleaner {
44	    private final Safelist safelist;
45	
46	    /**
47	     Create a new cleaner, that sanitizes documents using the supplied safelist.
48	     @param safelist safe-list to clean with
49	     */
